---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { AUDIT_DATE, AUDIT_DATE_ISO, officialUrl, taglines } from '../content/site';
import { alternatives } from '../content/alternatives';
import { itemList, webPage } from '../lib/schema';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const canonical = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href;
const origin = Astro.site!.origin;
---

<Base
  title="Client Portal Alternatives for Agencies: Self-Hosted vs Hosted"
  description="Honest comparisons of ClientOS against Wayfront (Service Provider Pro), Zendo and Agency Handy — built from each vendor's own published pricing, with the date each was checked."
  og="og/alternatives.png"
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: `${base}/` }, { label: 'Alternatives' }]}
  schema={[
    webPage({
      url: canonical,
      name: 'Client portal alternatives for agencies',
      description:
        'Comparisons of ClientOS against hosted client portals for productized service agencies.',
      modified: AUDIT_DATE_ISO,
    }),
    itemList(
      'Client portal comparisons',
      alternatives.map((a) => ({
        name: `${a.product} alternative`,
        url: `${origin}${base}/${a.slug}/`,
      })),
    ),
  ]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Alternatives</p>
      <h1>Hosted client portal, or one you own?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every portal on this page does roughly the same job: a storefront for your services, a place
        for clients to order and talk to you, and invoices at the end. They split on one structural
        question — whose server it runs on — and the pricing model follows from that.
      </p>
      <p class="meta" style="margin-top:1.25rem">{taglines[1]}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>How these comparisons are built, and why you can check them.</strong> We make
          ClientOS, so we are not a neutral party and we are not pretending to be. What we can do is
          show our working: every competitor number is read off that competitor’s own public pricing
          page, with the date it was read, and every ClientOS claim comes from getclientos.com or
          its docs. Each page also names the agencies that should stay where they are. Where a
          vendor’s pages could not be reached, we say so and make no claim at all rather than
          guessing — see
          <a href="https://github.com/huelab-byte/tryclientos/blob/main/UNVERIFIED.md" rel="noopener">
            UNVERIFIED.md</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
        {
          alternatives.map((a) => (
            <a class="card card-link" href={`${base}/${a.slug}/`}>
              <h3>ClientOS vs {a.product}</h3>
              <p>{a.description}</p>
              <span class="card-more">
                {a.verified
                  ? `Pricing checked ${a.checked} →`
                  : 'Model comparison — their terms unverified →'}
              </span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-raised">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The structural difference, in one table</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        This is the part that is true of every hosted portal versus every self-hosted one, whichever
        two products you are comparing.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <table>
          <caption>
            Hosted subscription portals versus ClientOS self-hosted. ClientOS column from
            getclientos.com, checked {AUDIT_DATE}. The hosted column describes the model, not any
            one vendor — see the individual pages for named, dated prices.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">&nbsp;</th>
              <th scope="col">Hosted portal subscription</th>
              <th scope="col" class="ours">ClientOS, self-hosted</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What you pay for</th>
              <td>A monthly plan that grows with seats, clients or features</td>
              <td class="ours">One licence, paid once, plus a server from about $10 a month</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Where client data lives</th>
              <td>On the vendor’s servers, under their terms</td>
              <td class="ours">In your own PostgreSQL database, on your server</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Who can read it</th>
              <td>You, the vendor’s staff and their subprocessors</td>
              <td class="ours">Only the people you give an account to</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Who runs the server</th>
              <td>They do — nothing for you to patch, monitor or back up</td>
              <td class="ours">
                You do. One command installs and updates it, but the VPS and the backup schedule
                are yours
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Payments</th>
              <td>Gateways and any cut depend on the vendor and the plan</td>
              <td class="ours">
                Straight to your Stripe, PayPal or crypto account; ClientOS takes no cut
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Branding</th>
              <td>Their domain or a paid add-on, and often their name somewhere</td>
              <td class="ours">Your domains, logos, colours, words and emails on every package</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Leaving</th>
              <td>Export what they let you, then lose the rest</td>
              <td class="ours">
                The database and files are already yours; one command dumps all of it
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Source code</th>
              <td>Not provided</td>
              <td class="ours">
                Not provided either — verified Docker images, so self-hosted but not open source
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="meta">
        The framing in this table is ClientOS’s own, from the "Why self-hosted" section of
        <a href={officialUrl('alternatives_hub', '/#own-it')} rel="noopener">
          getclientos.com</a>; the two rows about running the server and about source code are added here because
        that section does not state them and a buyer should know both.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>When a hosted portal is the right answer</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Self-hosting is not free — it costs you a server to own and a backup schedule to remember.
        Stay on a hosted portal if any of these are true:
      </p>
      <ul class="checks" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <li>Nobody on your team wants to own a Linux VPS, and you would rather pay to never think about it.</li>
        <li>You need the portal in a language other than English — ClientOS states its interface is English only.</li>
        <li>You want to use the real product free before committing; ClientOS sells a licence and its demo is read-only for 3 days.</li>
        <li>You are one or two people, where a cheap per-seat plan beats a licence.</li>
        <li>You depend on an integration ClientOS does not list, such as Zapier, Make, Slack or SEO reporting.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        The curated
        <a href={`${base}/client-portal-software/`}>list of client portal software</a> has the hosted
        options, listed neutrally and without ranking.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta utm="alternatives_hub" />
</Base>
